(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-classes-2027q-007"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-classes-2027q-007","title":"DVRUO Classes GATE CS | C-Programming | Functions, Storage classes & Loops | Weekly Quiz 2","sourceTitle":"DVRUO Classes GATE CS | C-Programming | Functions, Storage classes & Loops | Weekly Quiz 2","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO CLASSES","providerKey":"classes","series":"DVRUO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027","seriesId":"cs-classes-2027q","num":7,"category":"other","year":"","format":"B","durationSec":2160,"info":{"orgName":"DVRUO Classes","batchName":"DVRUO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027","subjectName":"C-Programming"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/GO CLASSES/GO Classes Weekly Quizzes for GATE CSE 2027/083_exam_964_GO Classes GATE CS C-Programming Functions, Storage classes & Loops Weekly Quiz 2Popular.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>As shown below, the file $f.c$ defines a function f whose code refers to the variable $x$ that is not a local variable or a parameter. The file $g.c$ defines a function $g$ whose code refers to the variable $x$ that is not a local variable or a parameter.</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">// file f.c\n// declaration for x\n\nvoid f(int a){\n    ...\n    x = ...\n}</pre><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">// file g.c\n// declaration for x\n\nvoid g(char* p){\n    ...\n    x = ...\n}</pre><p>The following table below shows how the variable x is defined in each file. For each row in the table, indicate in Column (c) whether the two functions at run-time refer to the SAME identifier (memory location) or to DIFFERENT identifiers (memory locations)<br>$$\\begin{array} {|l|l|c|} \\hline&nbsp;\\qquad \\textbf{Column A}&nbsp;&amp; \\qquad \\textbf{Column B} &amp; \\textbf{Column C} \\\\&nbsp;\\textbf{declaration in f.c} &amp;&nbsp;\\textbf{declaration in g.c} &amp; \\textbf{SAME or DIFFERENT} \\\\\\hline&nbsp; \\text{int x;} &amp; \\text{extern int x;} &amp; {\\color{Red} {\\textbf{SAME}}} \\\\\\hline&nbsp;&nbsp; \\text{int x = 0;} &amp; \\text{static int x;} &amp; \\\\\\hline \\text{static int x;} &amp; \\text{static int x;} &amp; \\\\\\hline \\text{static int x;} &amp; \\text{extern int x = 0;} &amp; \\\\\\hline &nbsp;\\end{array}$$</p><p>The first row of column C has been filled for hints. What will be in the $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th row of Column C, respectively?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>SAME, DIFFERENT, DIFFERENT</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>SAME, SAME, SAME</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>DIFFERENT, DIFFERENT, DIFFERENT</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>DIFFERENT, SAME, DIFFERENT</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 32)</small></h4><a name=\"404523\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong><em>1.int x;</em></strong></p>\n\n<p>Is a global variable stored in static memory.</p>\n\n<p><strong><em>extern int x;</em></strong></p>\n\n<p>Is an indication of the presence of a global variable x either in the same or a linked file.</p>\n\n<p>Since f.c has a declaration of x variable, g.c can access this x from f.c</p>\n\n<p>Hence, both the files are referring to the same identifier.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong><em>2.int x=0;</em></strong></p>\n\n<p>Is a definition of a global variable stored in static memory.</p>\n\n<p><strong><em>static int x;</em></strong></p>\n\n<p>Is a static variable also stored in static memory. Although it is declared globally, static variables&nbsp;are&nbsp;not available to linker. They are&nbsp;available only to the functions of the same file. So, both the files are not referring to the same variable.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong><em>3.static int x;</em></strong></p>\n\n<p>Is a static variable declared globally stored in static memory.</p>\n\n<p><strong><em>static int x; </em></strong><em>of g.c</em></p>\n\n<p>Is also stored in static memory, but both the variables are only available in their respective files.</p>\n\n<p>So, they aren’t referring to the same variable.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong><em>4.static int x;</em></strong></p>\n\n<p>Is a static variable declared globally stored in static memory.</p>\n\n<p><strong><em>extern int x=0;</em></strong></p>\n\n<p>Which is <strong><em>extern int x;</em></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; int x=0;</em></strong></p>\n\n<p>Is referring to the global variable and is followed by its definition. Its different from the static variable in f.c</p>\n\n<p>So, both the files aren’t referring to the same variable.</p>\n\n<p><strong>OPTION C- DIFFERENT, DIFFERENT, DIFFERENT</strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"405879\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Option C is correct as,</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>int x = 0;&nbsp; is defined globally and static int = 0; is also defined globally but not available to the linker<strong>.[DIFFERENT]</strong></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>static int x; of both functions are defined globally but both the variables are available only to respective files.<strong>[DIFFERENT]</strong></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>static int x; is defined globally&nbsp;with no linker but&nbsp;extern int x = 0; is basically extern int; int x = 0; having the functionality of linker. <strong>[DIFFERENT]</strong></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"530922\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>In both files,&nbsp;<code>x</code>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<strong>not local</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>not a parameter</strong>, so it must be a&nbsp;<strong>global/static variable</strong>.</p><p>We must decide whether both functions refer to the:</p><ul><li><p><strong>SAME memory location</strong><br>or</p></li><li><p><strong>DIFFERENT memory locations</strong></p></li></ul><hr><h1 id=\"important-c-concepts\">Important C Concepts</h1><h2 id=\"1.-int-x;\">1. int x;</h2><p>Global variable with&nbsp;<strong>external linkage</strong>&nbsp;by default.</p><p>Meaning:</p><ul><li><p>Other files can access the same variable using&nbsp;<code>extern</code>.</p></li></ul><hr><h2 id=\"2.-extern-int-x;\">2. extern int x;</h2><p>This does&nbsp;<strong>not create</strong>&nbsp;a new variable.</p><p>It only says:</p><blockquote><p>“The variable exists somewhere else.”</p></blockquote><p>So it refers to an already existing global variable.</p><hr><h2 id=\"3.-static-int-x;\">3. static int x;</h2><p>Global variable with&nbsp;<strong>internal linkage</strong>.</p><p>Meaning:</p><ul><li><p>Visible only inside that file.</p></li><li><p>Another file cannot access it.</p></li><li><p>Each file gets its&nbsp;<strong>own separate copy</strong>.</p></li></ul><hr><h1 id=\"row-by-row-explanation\">Row-by-row Explanation</h1><hr><h1 id=\"row-1\">Row 1</h1><h3 id=\"in-f.c\">In f.c</h3><pre><span style=\"color:#f1c40f;\"><code>int x;\n</code></span></pre><h3 id=\"in-g.c\">In g.c</h3><pre><span style=\"color:#27ae60;\"><code>extern int x;\n</code></span></pre><p><code>int x;</code>&nbsp;creates the variable.</p><p><code>extern int x;</code>&nbsp;refers to that same variable.</p><p>So both functions use the&nbsp;<strong>same memory location</strong>.</p><p>✅ Answer:&nbsp;<strong>SAME</strong></p><p>(Already given)</p><hr><h1 id=\"row-2\">Row 2</h1><h3 id=\"in-f.c-2\">In f.c</h3><pre><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\"><code>int x = 0;\n</code></span></pre><h3 id=\"in-g.c-2\">In g.c</h3><pre><span style=\"color:#2980b9;\"><code>static int x;\n</code></span></pre><p>Analysis:</p><ul><li><p><code>int x = 0;</code>&nbsp;→ global variable with external linkage</p></li><li><p><code>static int x;</code>&nbsp;→ separate internal variable only for&nbsp;<code>g.c</code></p></li></ul><p>These are two separate variables.</p><p>So memory locations are different.</p><p>✅ Answer:&nbsp;<strong>DIFFERENT</strong></p><hr><h1 id=\"row-3\">Row 3</h1><h3 id=\"in-f.c-3\">In f.c</h3><pre><span style=\"color:#e67e22;\"><code>static int x;\n</code></span></pre><h3 id=\"in-g.c-3\">In g.c</h3><pre><span style=\"color:#9b59b6;\"><code>static int x;\n</code></span></pre><p>Both are&nbsp;<code>static</code>.</p><p>That means:</p><ul><li><p><code>f.c</code>&nbsp;has its own private&nbsp;<code>x</code></p></li><li><p><code>g.c</code>&nbsp;has its own private&nbsp;<code>x</code></p></li></ul><p>They cannot see each other.</p><p>So they are separate memory locations.</p><p>✅ Answer:&nbsp;<strong>DIFFERENT</strong></p><hr><h1 id=\"row-4\">Row 4</h1><h3 id=\"in-f.c-4\">In f.c</h3><pre><span style=\"color:#3498db;\"><code>static int x;\n</code></span></pre><h3 id=\"in-g.c-4\">In g.c</h3><pre><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\"><code>extern int x = 0;\n</code></span></pre><p>First understand:</p><p><code>static int x;</code><br>→ internal to&nbsp;<code>f.c</code><br>→ cannot be accessed outside&nbsp;<code>f.c</code></p><p>Now in&nbsp;<code>g.c</code>:</p><pre><code>extern int x = 0;\n</code></pre><p>Because it has initialization (<code>=0</code>), this actually becomes a&nbsp;<strong>definition</strong>&nbsp;of a new global variable.</p><p>It is NOT referring to the static variable in&nbsp;<code>f.c</code>.</p><p>So:</p><ul><li><p><code>f.c</code>&nbsp;has one private static&nbsp;<code>x</code></p></li><li><p><code>g.c</code>&nbsp;creates another global&nbsp;<code>x</code></p></li></ul><p>Different memory locations.</p><p>✅ Answer:&nbsp;<strong>DIFFERENT</strong></p><hr><h1 id=\"final-answers\">Final Answers</h1><table><thead><tr><th>Row</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2</td><td>DIFFERENT</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>DIFFERENT</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>DIFFERENT</td></tr></tbody></table><hr><h1 id=\"correct-option\">Correct Option</h1><p>✅&nbsp;<strong>Option C</strong></p><p>{DIFFERENT, DIFFERENT, DIFFERENT}<br><br><br><span style=\"background-color:#f1c40f;\">(this answer is from chatgpt , it helped me to understand better .hope it helps others as well)</span></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"533688\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Based on the question, we can tell that<strong><span style=\"background-color:#f1c40f;\"> file f.c and g.c are linked together berfore executing</span></strong>&nbsp;, otherwise case-1 of the table won't be possible&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; table-layout: fixed;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"background: rgb(45, 122, 79); color: rgb(255, 255, 255); padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center; border: 1px solid rgb(45, 122, 79); vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">Column&nbsp;A<br>declaration&nbsp;in&nbsp;f.c</span></th><th style=\"background: rgb(45, 122, 79); color: rgb(255, 255, 255); padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center; border: 1px solid rgb(45, 122, 79); vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">Column&nbsp;B<br>declaration&nbsp;in&nbsp;g.c</span></th><th style=\"background: rgb(45, 122, 79); color: rgb(255, 255, 255); padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center; border: 1px solid rgb(45, 122, 79); vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">Column&nbsp;C<br>SAME&nbsp;or&nbsp;DIFFERENT</span></th><th style=\"background: rgb(45, 122, 79); color: rgb(255, 255, 255); padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center; border: 1px solid rgb(45, 122, 79); vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">Reason</span></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; height: 37.4583px; vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">int&nbsp;x;</span></td><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; height: 37.4583px; vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">extern int&nbsp;x;</span></td><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; height: 37.4583px; vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">SAME</span></td><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; height: 37.4583px; vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">f will use the global x and g is also refering to the same global x due to extern,</span></td></tr><tr style=\"background:#edf7f1;\"><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">int&nbsp;x=0;</span></td><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">static int&nbsp;x;</span></td><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; vertical-align: middle;\"><strong><span style=\"color:#2ecc71;\">DIFFERENT</span></strong></td><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">f will refer to the global x and g will refer to the static x(lifetime within file g and not available to the linker)</span></td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">static int&nbsp;x;</span></td><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">static int&nbsp;x;</span></td><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; vertical-align: middle;\"><strong><span style=\"color:#2ecc71;\">DIFFERENT</span></strong></td><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">Both will refer to their static x within their file&nbsp;(lifetime within respective file&nbsp;and not available to the linker)</span></td></tr><tr style=\"background:#edf7f1;\"><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">static int&nbsp;x;</span></td><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\"><span style=\"background-color:#f1c40f;\">extern&nbsp;int&nbsp;x=0;&nbsp;</span></span><br>&nbsp;</td><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; vertical-align: middle;\"><strong><span style=\"color:#2ecc71;\">DIFFERENT</span></strong></td><td style=\"padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgb(200, 230, 211); text-align: center; vertical-align: middle;\"><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">f will refer to its static x and g will refer to it's global x.</span></td></tr></tbody></table><hr><p>NOTE:</p><p>Writing&nbsp;<span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\"><code>extern int x;</code>&nbsp;</span>is equivalent to :</p><ol><li><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">extern int x;</span></li><li><span style=\"color:#e74c3c;\">int x=0;</span></li></ol></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"527892\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">DIFFERENT , DIFFERENT , DIFFERENT</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404469\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the below program written into $2$ files.</p><p><strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"349\" width=\"624\" src=\"media/00/0096cdae9d01f244d759.png\"></strong></p><p>Note that compilation of&nbsp;f$1$.c and f$2$.c happens independently of each other.</p><p>Which of the following is TRUE?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>f$1$.c and f$2$.c can be compiled independently.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>f$1$.c will produce a compilation error since variable $x$ does not get any memory, and we are using it in printf.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>f$1$.c and f$2$.c can be compiled and linked. The output of the program will be $00.$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>f$2$.c will produce a compilation error since function fun() is not defined in&nbsp;f$2$.c.</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 40)</small></h4><a name=\"374480\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Answer: A</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>A: </strong>Extern variable is just a declaration whose definition is looked for during linking phase. So compiling both files individually causes no issues.</p>\n\n<p><strong>B: </strong>This is true that x does not get any memory because this is just a declaration, however this will never cause compilation error. This <strong>may</strong> get linker error later on.</p>\n\n<p><strong>C: </strong>These can be compiled but can not be linked because in f2.c the definition of variable x is of type static, and static variables are not available to the linker. So the x which f1.c was looking for is never found.</p>\n\n<p><strong>D: </strong>Since the prototype of fun() is available with f2.c there will never be compilation error. Again there <strong>may</strong> be linker error later on if the definition is not found.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 5)</small></h4><a name=\"447824\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><ul><li>In&nbsp;<strong>f1.c:&nbsp;</strong>The declaration&nbsp;<strong>extern int x;</strong><strong>&nbsp;</strong>states&nbsp;that the variable&nbsp;<strong>x&nbsp;</strong>is&nbsp;<strong>globally defined</strong>&nbsp;in another file. However, the&nbsp;compiler will&nbsp;look for the definition of&nbsp;<strong>x&nbsp;</strong>during the linking stage.&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</li><li>In&nbsp;<strong>f2.c:&nbsp;</strong>The declaration&nbsp;<strong>static int x;&nbsp;</strong>states&nbsp;that the variable&nbsp;<strong>x&nbsp;</strong>is defined as static, limiting its&nbsp;scope to&nbsp;<strong>f2.c</strong>. This means&nbsp;that the variable&nbsp;<strong>x&nbsp;</strong>even though defined globally within&nbsp;<strong>f2.c</strong>, cannot be accessed by other files (In this case&nbsp;<strong>f1.c</strong>). Because Static Storage Class limits its accessibility to the file/block it is declared in.&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</li><li>The function&nbsp;<strong>fun()&nbsp;</strong>is declared as&nbsp;<strong>void fun();&nbsp;</strong>in&nbsp;<strong>f2.c&nbsp;</strong>but it is not defined within this file. The compiler treats&nbsp;it as an&nbsp;<strong>externally defined function,&nbsp;</strong>meaning its definition (implementation of the function)&nbsp;will be found in another file (In this case,&nbsp;<strong>f1.c</strong>).&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</li><li>Both&nbsp;<strong>f1.c&nbsp;</strong>and&nbsp;<strong>f2.c&nbsp;</strong>will compile independently without errors because the&nbsp;<strong>compiler only checks for syntax and semantics within each line. It doesn't check for cross-file dependencies</strong>&nbsp;during the compilation stage.&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</li><li>During the<strong>&nbsp;​​​​linking stage,&nbsp;</strong>the linker attempts to resolve all the cross-file dependencies such as references to&nbsp;<strong>functions or variables declared as extern in one file and defined in another</strong>.&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</li><li>When the function&nbsp;<strong>fun()&nbsp;</strong>is called, the linker successfully resolves this reference because<strong>&nbsp;fun()&nbsp;</strong>is defined in&nbsp;<strong>f1.c</strong>.&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</li><li>Although&nbsp;the function&nbsp;<strong>fun()&nbsp;</strong>is correctly linked, the&nbsp;<strong>print()&nbsp;</strong>statement inside&nbsp;<strong>fun()&nbsp;</strong>requires the value of&nbsp;<strong>x,&nbsp;</strong>which is declared as&nbsp;<strong>extern&nbsp;</strong>in&nbsp;<strong>f1.c</strong>.<strong>&nbsp;</strong>Since&nbsp;<strong>x&nbsp;</strong>is declared as static in&nbsp;<strong>f2.c,&nbsp;</strong>it is not globally accessible. As a result,&nbsp;the&nbsp;<strong>linker cannot find a global definition of x&nbsp;</strong>, leading to a&nbsp;<strong>Linker error.&nbsp;</strong></li></ul></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"494773\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>every variable and function used have their respective definations in their file therefore there is no problem in compilation. extern x tells the compiler that there might be a int variable x present in the file without any defination &nbsp;but dont throw an error as this defination might be present after the main or &nbsp;some other file . &nbsp;Same happens in f2.c where void fun(); is a declaration that fun might be present later <strong>or in some other file</strong> don't throw any compiler error .&nbsp;</p><p>But during linking phase as the only x defination prsesnt is in f2.c which is static therefore not availabe to the linker so extern can't point to that variable hence linking error.</p><p>One important variation could be that if in f2.c there is int x; instead of static int x; then there would be no linking error due to x. Functions which are not static in nature are also availabe to the linker therefore it will also not throw linker error hence both will be linked successfully .</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/374455\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":3,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider a function is_greater() given below. Mark all option(s) which return $1$.</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">int is_greater(unsigned int x, unsigned int y)\n{\n    if(x-y &gt; 0)\n        return 1;\n    else \n        return 0;\n}\n</pre>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>is_greater$(1, 2)$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>is_greater$(-1, -2)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>is_greater$(-2, -1)$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>is_greater$(-1, 0)$</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, C, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Selected solution <small>(score: 47)</small></h4><a name=\"373133\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><h2 style=\"font-style:italic;\"><strong>ans-A,B,C,D</strong></h2>\n\n<h2 style=\"font-style:italic;\">given&nbsp; x and y are unsigned&nbsp; the difference between them is also unsigned</h2>\n\n<h2 style=\"font-style:italic;\">and greater than 0;</h2></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 21)</small></h4><a name=\"373142\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>All the options are correct .</p>\n\n<p>Now first try to learn how unsigned numbers are represented in the computer when we pass negative number&nbsp;as argument and it catch as an unsigned number.</p>\n\n<p>Say My computer is using 32 bit to store an integer datatype.</p>\n\n<p>now using 32 bits the largest unsigned number we can represent&nbsp;$2^{32}-1=4294967295$</p>\n\n<p>now signed number was represented 2’s complement form in computer.</p>\n\n<p>now 32 bit largest number we can represent :-$2^{31}-1=2147483647$</p>\n\n<p>now how -1 is represented in signed form ?</p>\n\n<p>=&gt;(11111111111111111111111111111111) which is in 2’s complement form evaluated to -1.</p>\n\n<p>now how -1 is evaluated in unsigned form?</p>\n\n<p>=&gt;(11111111111111111111111111111111) which evaluated to&nbsp;4294967295.</p>\n\n<p>same how -2 is is evaluated in unsigned form?</p>\n\n<p>=&gt;&nbsp;(11111111111111111111111111111110) which evaluated to&nbsp;4294967294.</p>\n\n<p>now one more point :-</p>\n\n<p>difference of two unsigned number:-&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>the result of subtraction&nbsp; is if comes negative we use modular arithmetic to convert it to unsigned&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>by the following rule (a-b)mod&nbsp;$2^{32}$ .</p>\n\n<p>for example we have a=2,b=4&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>now a-b =-2 which will represent as&nbsp;$((-2)mod 2^{32})$=4294967294</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>one might read this article for more info:-&nbsp;<a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/7221409/is-unsigned-integer-subtraction-defined-behavior\" target=\"_blank\">https://stackoverflow.com/questions/7221409/is-unsigned-integer-subtraction-defined-behavior</a></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>now coming to the following question:-&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>option a:-</p>\n\n<p>is_greater(1,2) is called.&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>now&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>x-y= -1 now result comes negative so it will represent as&nbsp;4294967295.</p>\n\n<p>which is greater than 0&nbsp;so 1 will be returned.</p>\n\n<p>option b:-</p>\n\n<p>is_greater(-1,-2) is called.&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>now x=(-1) is represented as&nbsp;4294967295.</p>\n\n<p>now y=(-2) is represented as&nbsp;4294967294.</p>\n\n<p>the difference is&nbsp;1 which is greater than 0.&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>so 1 will be returned.</p>\n\n<p>option c:</p>\n\n<p>is_greater(−2,−1) is called.</p>\n\n<p>now x=(-2) is represented as&nbsp;4294967294.</p>\n\n<p>now y=(-1) is represented as&nbsp;4294967295.</p>\n\n<p>now x-y =-1 which again converted to&nbsp;$((-1)mod 2^{32})=4294967295.$ which is greater than 0 , so 1 will be returned.</p>\n\n<p>option D:-</p>\n\n<p>I believe now you can do it by yourself.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"440800\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><ul><li>&nbsp;The thing is that (x - y) returns a unsigned integer. That's it.</li><li>&nbsp;Further, Explanation is that when you do a subtraction let's say (1,2) and you get \"-1\" this is implicitly converted to a unsigned integer cause of the expression (x-y) and this returns a implicit unsigned value.</li><li>&nbsp;So this -1 is converted to \"1111 1111 1111 1111 1111 1111 1111 1111\" which is the 2's complement of this but since it is unsigned it would return 2^32 - 1.</li><li>&nbsp;And since the only possible way for x - y = 0 is when x = y.</li><li>&nbsp;In all the other cases, due to the return of an unsigned value it will be always greater than zero.</li><li>So in all possible options the value of return in decimal is either negative or positive so it will always be something of big in the unsigned and a pattern of (a - b) mod 2^32</li></ul></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"373166\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><hr>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=9247874147895723948\" target=\"_blank\">https://gateoverflow.in/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=9247874147895723948</a></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"373141\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">All coz in unsigned -1 is the largest value so obviously it would be greater than 0</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 6 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"437471\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>All options return 1 because the function uses unsigned integers, where negative values are treated as large positive numbers due to wrap-around. In unsigned arithmetic:</p><ul><li>is_greater(1, 2) results in a large positive value from 1 - 2.</li><li>is_greater(-1, -2) and is_greater(-2, -1) both involve large unsigned values (UINT_MAX and UINT_MAX - 1).</li><li>is_greater(-1, 0) similarly involves -1 treated as UINT_MAX.</li></ul><p><br>In all cases, the subtraction yields a value greater than 0.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/373103\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":4,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following(s) will evaluate to true</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$0\\; \\&\\&\\; 0 == 0$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$0\\; \\&\\&\\; 1 == 0$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$1\\; ||\\; 0 == 0$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$1\\; ||\\; 1 == 0$</p>"}],"answer":["C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 40)</small></h4><a name=\"373104\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Answer: C, D</p>\n\n<p>Note that precedence and associativity tells us how the expression is parenthesized.<br>\nThen the expression is evaluated in the evaluation order in this case it is from left to right.</p>\n\n<p>(A)&nbsp; Expression $0 \\&amp;\\&amp; 0==0$ is parenthesized as $0 \\&amp;\\&amp; (0==0)$ but when it is evaluated it is evaluated from left to right, since we get $0$ from the first expression due to short-circuiting in C&nbsp;we get $0$ and further evaluation will not take place.</p>\n\n<p>Similarly in case of (B) we get 0.</p>\n\n<p>(C)&nbsp;Expression $1&nbsp;||&nbsp;0==0$ is parenthesized as $1&nbsp;|| (0==0)$ but when it is evaluated it is evaluated from left to right, since we get $1$ from the first expression due to short-circuiting in C&nbsp;we get $1$ and further evaluation will not take place.</p>\n\n<p>Similarly in case of (D) we get 1.</p>\n\n<hr>\n<p>Now considering examples given by @shishir_roy,</p>\n\n<p><br>\n$1||0,0$ is parenthesized as $(1||0),0$ and here short-circuiting will happen while calculating $(1||0)$ and $1$ will be returned. Now in case of comma operator rightmost expression value is returned so in our case $0$ will be returned hence we get $1||0,0=0$.</p>\n\n<p>Similarly work it out for $0\\&amp;\\&amp;1, 1$.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"497788\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"\" width=\"600\" height=\"210\" src=\"media/98/98c6842a136279efa86b.jpg\"></p><p><strong>Answer: Option C) &amp; D)</strong></p><p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"484261\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><h3 data-start=\"140\" data-end=\"181\">C Operator Precedence (high → low)</h3><ol data-start=\"182\" data-end=\"278\"><li data-start=\"182\" data-end=\"223\"><p data-start=\"185\" data-end=\"223\"><strong data-start=\"185\" data-end=\"200\"><code data-start=\"187\" data-end=\"191\">==</code> , <code data-start=\"194\" data-end=\"198\">!=</code></strong> → Equality operators</p></li><li data-start=\"224\" data-end=\"251\"><p data-start=\"227\" data-end=\"251\"><strong data-start=\"227\" data-end=\"235\"><code data-start=\"229\" data-end=\"233\">&amp;&amp;</code></strong> → Logical AND</p></li><li data-start=\"252\" data-end=\"278\"><p data-start=\"255\" data-end=\"278\"><strong data-start=\"255\" data-end=\"263\"><code data-start=\"257\" data-end=\"261\">||</code></strong> → Logical OR</p></li></ol></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"526872\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>For logical AND, OR we always evaluate using order of evaluation which is left to right, no matter what the precedence is (Reference <strong>Go</strong> <strong>classes</strong> <strong>lecture</strong> <strong>4e</strong>)</p><p>So here&nbsp;</p><p>A: False 0&amp;&amp;anything&nbsp;</p><p>B: False 0&amp;&amp;anything</p><p>C:True 1 || anything&nbsp;</p><p>D:True 1 || anything</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Another example&nbsp;</p><p>int i=1</p><p>if(i++ &amp;&amp; (i==1))</p><p>Print(Yes)</p><p>Else print (No)</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Here although the precedence for bracket is higher,that means, (i==1) should be evaluated first, still i++ will be evaluated first because orderof evaluation is from left to right&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/373101\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>What will be the output printed by the following program?</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\nint main()\n{\n    int i = 1;\n    for(i = 0; i&lt;10; i+3)\n    switch(i)\n    {\n        case 3:\n            printf(\"Hi. This is case 3\");\n            break;\n        case 6:\n            printf(\"Hi. This is case 6\");\n            break;\n            break;\n        default:\n            printf(\"Hi. This is default\");\n            break;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n</pre>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Hi. This is case $3$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Hi. This is case $6$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Infinite Execution</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Hi. This is default</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Selected solution <small>(score: 25)</small></h4><a name=\"373105\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Answer: C</strong></p><p>Since in the for loop $i$ is not updating it will keep on going inside the loop and the loop will never terminate.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"484262\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>for(i = 0; i &lt; 10; i + 3)</p><ul><li data-start=\"126\" data-end=\"187\"><p data-start=\"128\" data-end=\"187\"><code data-start=\"128\" data-end=\"135\">i + 3</code> is just an <strong data-start=\"147\" data-end=\"161\">expression</strong>, it doesn’t change <code data-start=\"181\" data-end=\"184\">i</code>.</p></li><li data-start=\"188\" data-end=\"234\"><p data-start=\"190\" data-end=\"234\">So <code data-start=\"193\" data-end=\"196\">i</code> stays <strong data-start=\"203\" data-end=\"216\">0 forever</strong>, infinite loop.</p></li></ul><div><p data-start=\"236\" data-end=\"277\">want to increment, you must write:</p><p><code>for(i = 0; i &lt; 10; i = i + 3)</code></p><blockquote data-start=\"921\" data-end=\"974\"><p data-start=\"923\" data-end=\"974\">if i put <code data-start=\"932\" data-end=\"943\">i = i + 3</code> means first they do 1, then 0?</p></blockquote><p data-start=\"976\" data-end=\"983\">No</p><ul data-start=\"984\" data-end=\"1048\"><li data-start=\"984\" data-end=\"1017\"><p data-start=\"986\" data-end=\"1017\">It starts at <code data-start=\"999\" data-end=\"1006\">i = 0</code> (not 1).</p></li></ul></div></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"477966\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Silly mistake prone question .<br>\nJust look at the increment statement</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"533585\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">In the For Loop we are incrementing i(i+3) but not assigning it to i .That's why value of i remains 0 forever.<br>\n<br>\nAnd break statement takes control out of switch statement as it's scope is limited to switch block.For loop contiues.<br>\n<br>\nOption C</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/373100\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>What will be the value printed by the following program?</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">int i = 10;\ni = !i &gt; 14;\nprintf(\"%d\", i);\n</pre>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$10$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$14$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$0$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$1$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C, E</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 17)</small></h4><a name=\"373111\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Answer: C</strong></p><p>$!$ has higher priority than $&gt;$,</p><p>$!10 $ will give false i.e. $0$ and $0&gt;14$ is also false so $i$ will store value 0, hence 0 will be printed.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 4)</small></h4><a name=\"373112\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>! is unary operator which has higher precedence than &gt; so first !i will be evaluated&nbsp; so !(10) which will give 0 as this is same as !(true) as all value other than 0 are considered as true then this will be evaluated 0&gt;14&nbsp; which is obviously false so&nbsp; 0 will be the answer <strong>option c</strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"477969\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Another , Silly mistake prone question .<br>\n! have heigher precedence , and any non zero value gives true<br>\nSo, !True =false i.e. 0<br>\n0&gt;14 again false i.e. 0<br>\nso 0</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"531889\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">i = 10<br>\n<br>\ni= ( ! i ) &gt; 14 <br>\n<br>\ni = ( ! 10) &gt;14 , &nbsp;means (! Non-zero) == 0(false)<br>\n<br>\ni = 0 &gt; 14 &nbsp;is false <br>\n<br>\ni beacome 0<br>\n<br>\nPF (\" 0\")<br>\n<br>\nOutput : 0 ( Answer C)</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/373099\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>What will be the output?</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">int a = 4, b =15, c =29;\nif(c&gt;b&gt;a)\n    printf(\"TRUE\");\nelse\n    printf(\"FALSE\");\n</pre>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{TRUE}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{FALSE}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Syntax Error</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Compilation Error</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C, B, A</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 15)</small></h4><a name=\"373106\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Answer: B</strong></p><p>Associativity of $&gt;$ is from left to right, so $c&gt;b&gt;a$ will be evaluated as $(c&gt;b)&gt;a$. Since $c&gt;b$ is true so expression will become $1&gt;a$ which is false. Hence we will go to else statement and FALSE will be printed.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 6)</small></h4><a name=\"373184\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>B is the answer.<br>\n&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/82/821062da081f1090097b.jpg\"></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 3)</small></h4><a name=\"373187\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>The answer is B.</strong></p>\n\n<p>Same precedence but associativity is <strong>left to right.</strong></p>\n\n<p>29 &gt;&nbsp;15 is evaluated to <strong>1</strong> (<strong>Boolean</strong>&nbsp;<strong>True)&nbsp;</strong>in signed&nbsp;integer type and 1&nbsp;&gt; 4 is again signed&nbsp;comparison which would evaluate to <strong>0 (Boolean False).</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>if(0) evaluates to FALSE hence,</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>else is executed and the final output is FALSE.</strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"533533\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>The correct option is B. FALSE.</p><hr><h2 role=\"heading\">🔑 The Core Concept: Left-to-Right Associativity</h2><p>In C, relational operators like <code dir=\"ltr\">&gt;</code> have left-to-right associativity. This means that a chained expression like <code dir=\"ltr\">c &gt; b &gt; a</code> does not evaluate as a mathematical range condition (like $c &gt; b$ and $b &gt; a$). Instead, the compiler evaluates it sequentially from left to right as <code dir=\"ltr\">(c &gt; b) &gt; a</code>.</p><hr><h2 role=\"heading\">🔍 Step-by-Step Evaluation</h2><p>Given variables: <code dir=\"ltr\">a = 4</code>, <code dir=\"ltr\">b = 15</code>, <code dir=\"ltr\">c = 29</code>.</p><ol><li>Evaluate the first relational expression (<code dir=\"ltr\">c &gt; b</code>):<ul><li>This simplifies to <code dir=\"ltr\">29 &gt; 15</code>.</li><li>Since 29 is greater than 15, this expression evaluates to true, which in C is represented by the integer value <code dir=\"ltr\">1</code>.</li></ul></li><li>Substitute that result back into the main condition:<ul><li><code dir=\"ltr\">(c &gt; b) &gt; a</code></li><li><code dir=\"ltr\">(1) &gt; a</code></li></ul></li><li>Evaluate the final expression (<code dir=\"ltr\">1 &gt; a</code>):<ul><li>This simplifies to <code dir=\"ltr\">1 &gt; 4</code>.</li><li>Since 1 is not greater than 4, this expression evaluates to false, represented by the integer value <code dir=\"ltr\">0</code>.</li></ul></li><li>Execute the conditional blocks:<ul><li>The <code dir=\"ltr\">if(0)</code> condition fails, skipping the <code dir=\"ltr\">printf(\"TRUE\");</code> block.</li><li>The execution falls through to the <code dir=\"ltr\">else</code> block, triggering <code dir=\"ltr\">printf(\"FALSE\");</code>.</li></ul></li></ol><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/373098\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p><br>If $x, y, z,$ and $w$ are declared as integer variables, which of the following expressions are $\\text{NOT}$ valid in $C$?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$x + 5 = y + z$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$x = y = z + w$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$x = ( z - y ) == w$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$w = x != y\\; \\&\\&\\; z$</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 18)</small></h4><a name=\"373120\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Option A is the correct answer.</p><p>For this statement we will get the error “</p><pre><strong>error: lvalue required as left operand of assignment</strong>” .</pre><p>Left side of the Expression is x+5 which is not allowed.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Option B is allowed in C.</p><p>$x=y=z+w$ it is same as</p><p>$y=z+w$;</p><p>$x=y$;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>option C is also allowed.</p><p>$x=(z-y)==w$ it can be written as;</p><p>$x=((z-y)==w) $ as $==$ has priority that $=.$</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Option D is also allowed.</p><p>$w=x!=y$ &amp;&amp; $z$ it can be written as,</p><p>$w=$($(x!=y)$&amp;&amp;$z$) .</p><p>so ,&nbsp;option a is the only option which is not valid in C.&nbsp;</p><p><strong>so correct answer is option A.</strong></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"373193\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>This is called a semantical error produced by compiler. The first option evaluates x+5 to be a constant, assigning anything to a constant is invalid.&nbsp;<br>\n&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Therefore <strong>Option A</strong> is the correct answer.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"477970\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Left Hand Side of assignment operator , can never be an expression .<br>\nTherefore Option A</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"533534\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>The expression that is NOT valid in C is A. x + 5 = y + z.</p><hr><h2 role=\"heading\">🔑 The Core Concept: Lvalues and Rvalues</h2><p>In C, the single equals sign (<code dir=\"ltr\">=</code>) is the assignment operator. It requires two components:</p><ul><li>Left-Hand Side (LHS): Must be a modifiable lvalue (a locator value that refers to a specific memory location where data can be stored, such as a variable name like <code dir=\"ltr\">x</code>).</li><li>Right-Hand Side (RHS): Can be any rvalue (a temporary value or expression that evaluates to a number, such as <code dir=\"ltr\">y + z</code>).</li></ul><hr><h2 role=\"heading\">❌ Option A: <code dir=\"ltr\">x + 5 = y + z</code> (Invalid)</h2><ul><li>The LHS is <code dir=\"ltr\">x + 5</code>. This is a mathematical expression that evaluates to a temporary rvalue, not a modifiable memory location.</li><li>You cannot assign a value to a temporary mathematical expression. Compiling this will throw a <code dir=\"ltr\">lvalue required as left operand of assignment</code> compilation error.</li></ul><h2 role=\"heading\">Option B: <code dir=\"ltr\">x = y = z + w</code> (Valid)</h2><ul><li>The assignment operator has right-to-left associativity.</li><li>This expression safely evaluates as <code dir=\"ltr\">x = (y = (z + w))</code>. The sum <code dir=\"ltr\">z + w</code> is assigned to variable <code dir=\"ltr\">y</code>, and that resulting value is then assigned to variable <code dir=\"ltr\">x</code>.</li></ul><h2 role=\"heading\">Option C: <code dir=\"ltr\">x = (z - y) == w</code> (Valid)</h2><ul><li>The equality operator (<code dir=\"ltr\">==</code>) has a higher precedence than the assignment operator (<code dir=\"ltr\">=</code>).</li><li>The compiler evaluates whether <code dir=\"ltr\">(z - y)</code> is equal to <code dir=\"ltr\">w</code>. This comparison results in either <code dir=\"ltr\">1</code> (true) or <code dir=\"ltr\">0</code> (false), which is then perfectly valid to store inside the variable <code dir=\"ltr\">x</code>.</li></ul><h2 role=\"heading\">Option D: <code dir=\"ltr\">w = x != y &amp;&amp; z</code> (Valid)</h2><ul><li>Operator precedence orders this expression as: <code dir=\"ltr\">w = ((x != y) &amp;&amp; z)</code>.</li><li>It first checks if <code dir=\"ltr\">x</code> is not equal to <code dir=\"ltr\">y</code>, performs a logical AND with <code dir=\"ltr\">z</code>, and assigns the final true/false result (<code dir=\"ltr\">1</code> or <code dir=\"ltr\">0</code>) to <code dir=\"ltr\">w</code>.</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"537678\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>The left side of <span style=\"font-family: monospace;\">=</span>&nbsp;must be a specific memory location, never a mathematical or logical expression.</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/373097\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>What will be the output of the following C program?.<br>Here $?:$ is a ternary operator which is right to left-associative.</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\nint main() {\n    int i =2, j =1;\n    if (--j ? ++j? i : i++ : i--)\n    printf(\"%d%d\\n\", i, j);\n}\n</pre>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$1\\; 0$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$2\\; 1$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$1\\; 1$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$2\\; 0$</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 12)</small></h4><a name=\"373118\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Answer :&nbsp;<strong>A. 1 0</strong></p>\n\n<p>Initially&nbsp;i = 2 and j = 1.</p>\n\n<p>Condition -&gt; --j ? ++j ? i : i++ : i-- which is equivalent</p>\n\n<p>to -&gt; --j ? [ ++j ? i : i++ ] : i--</p>\n\n<p>First --j is evaluated which makes j = 0 and returns 0 (false); therefore the condition is false so i-- is evaluated next which returns 2 (true) and makes i = 1</p>\n\n<p>So we print (i,j) -&gt; 1 0&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 12)</small></h4><a name=\"379003\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>One of the easy method to solve these types of questions is to convert ternary into if-else .</p>\n\n<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">if( --j ? ++j ? i : i++ : i--);</pre>\n\n<p>This statement can be rewritten as :</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">if (--j)\n{ \n    if(++j)\n    {\n        i;\n    }\n    else {\n        i++;\n    }\n}\nelse {\n    i--;\n}</pre>\n\n<p>Now if you dry run this code you will get i=1 , j=0</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"533535\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Common Misconception!</strong> Placing an expression inside an <code dir=\"ltr\">if</code> condition does not protect variables from being changed.&nbsp;</p><p><strong>NOTE: </strong>Any side-effects like modifications, increments (<code dir=\"ltr\">++</code>), or decrements (<code dir=\"ltr\">--</code>) will definitely modify the variables in memory as long as that specific part of the code gets evaluated.</p><hr><p>The conditional operator (<code dir=\"ltr\">? :</code>) has right-to-left associativity. The nested expression inside the <code dir=\"ltr\">if</code> statement is parsed by the compiler like this:</p><p>$$\\text{- -j } ? \\ (\\text{++j } ? \\ \\text{i} : \\text{i++}) : \\text{i- -}$$</p><hr><p>Initially, the variables are: <code dir=\"ltr\">i = 2</code>, <code dir=\"ltr\">j = 1</code>.</p><p><strong>Step A: Evaluate the main condition (<code dir=\"ltr\">--j</code>)</strong></p><ul><li><code dir=\"ltr\">--j</code> is a pre-decrement operator.</li><li>It modifies <code dir=\"ltr\">j</code> in memory immediately: <code dir=\"ltr\">j</code> drops from <code dir=\"ltr\">1</code> to <code dir=\"ltr\">0</code>.</li><li>The value returned by this expression is the new value, which is <code dir=\"ltr\">0</code> (False).</li></ul><p><strong>Step B: Follow the conditional branches (Short-Circuiting)</strong></p><ul><li>Because the condition evaluated to <code dir=\"ltr\">0</code> (False), the compiler completely skips the true branch: <code dir=\"ltr\">(++j ? i : i++)</code>.</li><li>This means <code dir=\"ltr\">++j</code> and <code dir=\"ltr\">i++</code> are never executed, so <code dir=\"ltr\">j</code> remains <code dir=\"ltr\">0</code>.</li><li>The execution jumps directly to the false branch: <code dir=\"ltr\">i--</code>.</li></ul><p><strong>Step C: Evaluate the false branch (<code dir=\"ltr\">i--</code>)</strong></p><ul><li><code dir=\"ltr\">i--</code> is a post-decrement operator.</li><li>The expression returns the <em>current</em> value of <code dir=\"ltr\">i</code> first, which is <code dir=\"ltr\">2</code>.</li><li>Right after returning that value, it decrements <code dir=\"ltr\">i</code> in memory: <code dir=\"ltr\">i</code> drops from <code dir=\"ltr\">2</code> to <code dir=\"ltr\">1</code>.</li></ul><hr><p><strong>🖨️ 3. Final Output Evaluation</strong></p><ul><li>The final value returned to the <code dir=\"ltr\">if</code> condition from the entire expression is <code dir=\"ltr\">2</code>.</li><li>Since <code dir=\"ltr\">2</code> is a non-zero value, the <code dir=\"ltr\">if</code> statement treats it as TRUE.</li><li>Because it is true, the program proceeds to run the inner statement: <code dir=\"ltr\">printf(\"%d%d\\n\", i, j);</code>.</li></ul><p>Printing the current values in memory:</p><ul><li><code dir=\"ltr\">i</code> is now <code dir=\"ltr\">1</code></li><li><code dir=\"ltr\">j</code> is now <code dir=\"ltr\">0</code></li></ul><p>Output: <code dir=\"ltr\">1 0</code></p><hr><p><img alt=\"\" width=\"488\" height=\"191\" src=\"media/32/32f7d4294e04f6cc0e7c.png\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"542366\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Answer :&nbsp;<strong>A</strong></p><ol><li>&nbsp;For the ternary operator (<strong> ? : </strong>) the associativity is fixed from <strong>R -&gt; L</strong>, and it's order of evaluation is also fixed from <strong>L -&gt; R</strong> just like Logical AND ( <strong>&amp;&amp;</strong> ), Logical OR ( <strong>||</strong><strong> </strong>), etc.</li><li>Hence associativity groups the expression like this :<br>&nbsp;<pre data-rawcode=\"--j ? ( ++j ? i : i++ ) : i--\" data-pbcklang=\"text\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\" data-linenums=\"true\" class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-text\">--j ? ( ++j ? i : i++ ) : i--</pre></li><li>But the evaluation happens from <strong>L -&gt; R</strong>, hence <strong>\"--j\"</strong> is evaluated first and based on the result of it <strong>\"i--\"</strong> is evaluated next.</li></ol><p>&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/373093\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>What is the value printed by the following program?</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">int main()\n{\n    int a = 0, b = 1, c = 0, d =-1;\n    d = (++a) &amp;&amp; (--b) &amp;&amp; (++c) ||(--a);\n    printf(\"%d\", d);\n}\n</pre>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$0$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$1$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$-1$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$2$</p>"}],"answer":[],"natRange":null,"answerText":"","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"solution_only\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 22)</small></h4><a name=\"373146\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/05/05927a3a34ff97b890ec.jpg\"></p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li>main()<br>\n\t{<br>\n\tint a=0,b=1,c=0, d=-1;<br>\n\td=(++a) &amp;&amp;(--b) &amp;&amp;(++c) ||(--a);<br>\n\tprintf(\"%d\", d);<br>\n\t}</li>\n</ol></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 5)</small></h4><a name=\"376017\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"8\">int main()\n{\n    int a = 0, b = 1, c = 0, d =-1;\n    d = (++a) &amp;&amp; (--b) &amp;&amp; (++c) ||(--a);\n    printf(\"%d\", d);\n}</pre>\n\n<p>To solve such a question where more than one operator is there in a single line, we use operator precedence and their associativity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Operator precedence</strong>:&nbsp;The precedence&nbsp;of the operator determines which operator is performed first in an expression with more than one operator with different precedence there.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Associativity</strong>: Associativity of the operator is used when two or more operators of the same precedence are present.</p>\n\n<p>Here following operator are use such as:</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>$() \\rightarrow \\text{Parentheses}$</li>\n\t<li>$++,--\\rightarrow \\text{Pre increment&nbsp; and Pre decrement Operator}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\&amp;\\&amp; \\rightarrow\\text{Logical AND}&nbsp;$</li>\n\t<li>$|| \\rightarrow \\text{Logical OR}$</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Among these $()$ has the highest priority and has left to right associativity. After that logical AND&nbsp;has higher precedence than logical OR, both these operators having left to right associativity.</p>\n\n<p>Order of evaluation : $()&gt;\\&amp;\\&amp;&gt;||$</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>d = (++a) &amp;&amp; (--b) &amp;&amp; (++c) ||(--a); here first solve&nbsp; (++a) &amp;&amp; (--b). both $a$ and $b$ are preincrement and predecrement operator respectively. after evaluating this expression $a=1,b=0$.</li>\n\t<li>now putting the value of $a,b$ we get&nbsp;$1\\&amp;\\&amp;0=0$ as output. as we see the expression become zero so next $\\&amp;\\&amp;$ operator is not executed therefore $c$ value is unchanged because of short circuit property of logical operator.</li>\n\t<li>Now $&nbsp;(((++a) \\&amp;\\&amp; (--b))\\&amp;\\&amp; (++c)) $ become $0$ so next $d=0||(--a)$ will be executed.</li>\n\t<li>since $a$ value is $1$ so when we pre decrement it becomes $0$.</li>\n\t<li>$d=0||0=0$, if both the input of logical OR gate is zero then output will be zero.</li>\n\t<li>Therefore the values of $d=0$&nbsp;&nbsp;</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><u><strong>Important points</strong></u>:</p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li><a href=\"https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/c-language/c-logical-operators?view=msvc-170\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">Short-circuiting property of logical operator</a>: while evaluating $\\&amp;\\&amp;$ operator if the left-hand side of the input is false then the right side of the part is never executed. similarly while evaluating the $||$ operator if the left-hand side of the input is true then the right side of the part is never be executed.</li>\n\t<li><a href=\"https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/c-language/precedence-and-order-of-evaluation?view=msvc-170\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">Precedence and order of evaluation</a></li>\n</ol></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"373151\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">According to priority order paranthesis is evaluated first and then &amp;&amp; and afterwards ||.<br>\n<br>\nThis results d=0.</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/373091\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>","keyMissing":true},{"n":11,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>The statement</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">while ( --counter &gt;= 1 )\ncounter % 2 ? printf(\"A\") : printf(\"B\");</pre><p>can $\\text{NOT}$ be rewritten as</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>&nbsp;&lt;pre class=&quot;prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp&quot; data-pbcklang=&quot;c_cpp&quot; data-pbcktabsize=&quot;4&quot;&gt;while ( --counter &gt;= 1 )\n    if ( counter % 2 )\n        printf(&quot;A&quot;);\n    else\n        printf(&quot;B&quot;);&lt;/pre&gt;</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>&nbsp;&lt;pre class=&quot;prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp&quot; data-pbcklang=&quot;c_cpp&quot; data-pbcktabsize=&quot;4&quot;&gt;while ( counter &gt;= 1 )\n    if (counter % 2)\n        printf(&quot;A&quot;);\n    else\n        printf(&quot;B&quot;);\n--counter;&lt;/pre&gt;</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>&nbsp;&lt;pre class=&quot;prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp&quot; data-pbcklang=&quot;c_cpp&quot; data-pbcktabsize=&quot;4&quot;&gt;while ( counter &gt; 1 )\n{\n    --counter;\n    if ( counter % 2 )\n        printf(&quot;A&quot;);\n    else\n        printf(&quot;B&quot;);\n}&lt;/pre&gt;</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>&nbsp;&lt;pre class=&quot;prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp&quot; data-pbcklang=&quot;c_cpp&quot; data-pbcktabsize=&quot;4&quot;&gt;do\n{\n    --counter;\n    printf( counter % 2 ? &quot;A&quot; : &quot;B&quot; );\n} while ( counter &gt;= 2 );&lt;/pre&gt;</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, D, A</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 31)</small></h4><a name=\"373114\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Answer :&nbsp;<strong>B, D</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Option A :</strong>&nbsp;while comparing given code and option A code, we can observe only the conditional operator statement is changed with if-else construct and rest is same. Even the if-else construct is doing same job as that of conditional operator statement.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong>Option B :&nbsp;</strong>In option B, we can observe the decrement operation is being done in last (and outside the loop body) as compared to original code where decrement is done in the beginning. This will produce different result, eg when counter is initialized to 2 given code will produce \"A\" as output whereas option B code will produce infinite loop.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong>Option C :</strong>&nbsp;In option C, we can observe the decrement operation is being done in the beginning of the loop body (which produces same effect). Also, the while condition is changed, so we need to check if both codes produces same result for all initialisations of counter.</p>\n\n<p>For counter = 1; given code does not enter the loop and option C code also does not enter the loop.</p>\n\n<p>For counter = 2; given code produces \"A\" as output and option C code also produces \"A\" as output.</p>\n\n<p>For counter = 3; given code produces \"BA\" as output and option C code also produces \"BA\" as output.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong>Option D :&nbsp;</strong>In option D, we can observe while loop (entry controlled) is changed to do while loop (exit controlled). Here, the loop body is same as that of option C code, except the if-else construct is converted into valid conditional operator.Also, the while condition is changed, so we need to check if both codes produces same result for all initialisations of counter.</p>\n\n<p>For counter = 3; given code produces \"BA\" as output and option D code also produces \"BA\" as output.</p>\n\n<p>For counter = 2; given code produces \"A\" as output and option D code also produces \"A\" as output.</p>\n\n<p>For counter = 1; given code does not enter the loop and option D code produces \"B\" as output.</p>\n\n<p>We can observe, option D code, for counter value less than equal to 1, will enter loop once.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"373185\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>B,D</strong> is the answer</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8f/8fdf1363f78747e4cc19.jpg\"></p>\n\n<p><br>\n&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"480579\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><pre data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\" class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\">&nbsp;\n</pre><p>Answer should be B,C,D</p><p>You can check why B,D are correct in above/below explanations.</p><p>C is also Correct.</p><p>When counter = 2,&nbsp;</p><pre data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\" class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\">while ( --counter &gt;= 1 )\n    counter % 2 ? printf(\"A\") : printf(\"B\");\n</pre><p>when the above code is executed, after the loop ends the value of counter is 0</p><p>but after the following code is excuted, at the end of the loop, the value of counter is 1</p><pre data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\" class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\">while ( counter &gt; 1 )\n{\n    --counter;\n    if ( counter % 2 )\n        printf(\"A\");\n    else\n        printf(\"B\");\n}\n</pre><pre data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\" class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\"></pre><p>Therefore they are not same.<span class=\"pupi_dm_user_mention\" data-pupi_dm_user_id=\"6166\"><a class=\"pupi_dm_user_mention\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/user/Sachin%20Mittal%201\" target=\"_blank\">@Sachin Mittal 1</a></span></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"493167\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><em>Just take 2 counter values that satisfies the given stmt and then compare it with given options and you&nbsp;</em></p><p><em>can&nbsp;easily eliminate right ones and choose wrong ones AS ANSWER as asked in the Qs.</em></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>eg.<strong> counter = 2 enters the loop and executes else part prints B</strong></p><p><strong>&nbsp; &nbsp; counter = 1 cannot enter the loop.</strong></p><p>this is <em>satisfied by options A,C&nbsp;</em></p><p>therefore <em><strong>Ans is B,D&nbsp;</strong></em></p><p><em><strong>p.s you can see B,D allows counter = 1 to enter the loop which can't be the case.</strong></em></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"533836\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><span style=\"background-color:#2ecc71;\">Answer: $B$,$D$</span></p><p>Detailed analysis:</p><p><img alt=\"\" width=\"286\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\" height=\"550\" src=\"denied:blob:https://gateoverflow.in/9e863ec4-2df4-4cdc-94dc-7e4dbe157119\"></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/373096\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":12,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Professor Greg wants to give assignments to his students to write a $C$ program for the $\\text{XOR5}$ function. $\\text{XOR5}$ is a function that returns true if and only if $\\text{EXACTLY}$ one of the integers $A$ and $B$ is $5$.<br>Using which of the following condition(s), $\\text{XOR5}$ can be implemented?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$(A==5) != (B==5)$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$!(A==5) != ! (B==5)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$(A==5) ? ! (B==5) : (B==5)$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$((A==5) || (B==5) )\\; \\&\\&\\; !((A==5)\\; \\&\\&\\; (B==5) )$</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, C, D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Selected solution <small>(score: 31)</small></h4><a name=\"404542\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">We can also solve this question using concepts from propositional logic.<br>\n<br>\nThe logic underlying the == operator is the biconditional logical operator($\\Leftrightarrow$).<br>\nSo, the != operator will be the negation of biconditional logical operator which is the xor logical operator($\\oplus$).<br>\n<br>\nNow, let $A==5$ be the proposition $P$ and $B==5$ be the proposition $Q$.<br>\n<br>\n$XOR5$ is the proposition $P \\oplus Q$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{A.}$ &nbsp;$P != Q$ $\\equiv P \\oplus Q$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\therefore$ &nbsp;$A.$ is correct.<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{B.}$ &nbsp;$!P != !Q$ $\\equiv \\neg P \\oplus \\neg Q \\equiv P \\oplus Q$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\therefore$ &nbsp;$B.$ is correct.<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{C.}$ &nbsp;$(P\\ ?\\ !Q : \\ Q)$ $\\equiv (P \\Rightarrow \\neg Q)\\land(\\neg P \\Rightarrow Q) \\equiv (\\neg P \\lor \\neg Q)\\land(P \\lor Q) \\equiv P \\oplus Q$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\therefore$ &nbsp;$C.$ is correct.<br>\n<br>\n$\\textbf{D.}$ &nbsp;$(P \\lor Q) \\land \\neg(P \\land Q) \\equiv (P \\lor Q) \\land (\\neg P \\lor \\neg Q)$ $\\equiv P \\oplus Q$.<br>\n<br>\n$\\therefore$ &nbsp;$D.$ is correct.</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 17)</small></h4><a name=\"373121\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Correct answer: A, B, C, D</strong></p>\n\n<p>Let’s see options one by one,</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\" type=\"A\">\n\t<li>$(A==5)!=(B==5)$ will return true only when one of the condition is true, if both are true and both are false it will return false. Hence this is a right option.</li>\n\t<li>It can also be seen as $(A!=5)!=(B!=5)$ it will return true when one of the condition is false, if both are false and both are true it will return false just as in option A. Hence this is also right option.</li>\n\t<li>Consider when $A==5$ it will return $!(B==5)$ if B is also 5 then it will return false else it will return true. Now if $A !=5$ it will return $(B==5)$ which only returns true when $B==5$. This is also a correct option.</li>\n\t<li>$((A==5)||(B==5))$ returns true if either one or both are equal to 5 but $!((A==5) \\;\\&amp;\\&amp;\\; (B==5))$&nbsp;returns false when both are equal to 5, and we know $T \\land F = F$. So this is also correct option.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"494945\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To solve in better way.</p><p>&nbsp;<img alt=\"\" width=\"254\" height=\"355\" src=\"media/bd/bdd57b492673a56edc69.jpg\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 0)</small></h4><a name=\"533847\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>There are 2 methods&nbsp;</p><ol><li>Method-1(Simply make the <span style=\"background-color:#f1c40f;\">TRUTH TABLE</span>)<br><br><img alt=\"\" width=\"284\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\" height=\"300\" src=\"denied:blob:https://gateoverflow.in/44d9acd1-816c-4179-9ae0-7c15654b6a3c\"></li><li><hr><p>Method-2(&nbsp;<span style=\"background-color: rgb(241, 196, 15);\">PROPOSTIONAL LOGIC</span>)<br><br><img alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"711\" src=\"denied:blob:https://gateoverflow.in/afd01229-960d-4f24-aba6-3280c3512a4f\"></p></li></ol><p>Answer: $A$$B$$C$$D$&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/373095\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":13,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let the variables $x,y,$ and $z$, be of type int and assigned some values. The variable $b1$ is initialized as follows:</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">int b1 = (x &gt; z) &amp;&amp; (y &gt;= z);</pre><p>Variables b2, b3, b4, and b5 are initialized below.</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">int b2 = (x &lt;= z) || (y &lt; z) \nint b3 = !((x &lt; z) || (y &lt;= z)) \nint b4 = !((x &lt; z) &amp;&amp; (y &gt;= z)) \nint b5 = ((x &gt; z) &amp;&amp; (y &gt; z)) || ((x &gt; z) &amp;&amp; (y == z))\n\n</pre><p>Variables $b2, b3, b4$, and $b5$ ALWAYS, SOMETIMES, or NEVER takes the same value as $b1$.<br>Which of the variable(s) SOMETIMES takes the same value as $b1$?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$b2$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$b3$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$b4$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$b5$</p>"}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, C</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Selected solution <small>(score: 39)</small></h4><a name=\"404546\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Let $P(a,b)$ be the predicate $a &gt; b$ and $Q(a,b)$ be the predicate&nbsp;$a \\ge&nbsp;b$.</p><p>$\\neg P(a,b)$ is $a \\le&nbsp;b$ and&nbsp;$\\neg Q(a,b)$ is $a &lt;&nbsp;b$.</p><p>We can rewrite $b1$, $b2$,…,&nbsp;$b5$ in terms of these predicates using propositional logic symbols.</p><p>The question asks whether $b1 \\Leftrightarrow&nbsp;bi$ is&nbsp;<em>Valid, Contingency or a Contradiction</em>.</p><p>$b1 \\equiv&nbsp;P(x,z) \\land Q(y, z)$</p><p>$b2 \\equiv&nbsp;\\neg P(x,z) \\lor \\neg Q(y,z) \\equiv \\neg b1$.</p><p>$b1 \\Leftrightarrow b2$ is a <em>Contradiction</em>.</p><p>So, $b2$&nbsp;<strong>NEVER</strong>&nbsp;takes the same value as $b1$.</p><hr><p>$b3 \\equiv \\neg(\\neg Q(x,z) \\lor \\neg P(y,z)) \\equiv P(y,z) \\land Q(x,z)$</p><p>Clearly this is not the same expression as $b1$&nbsp;but it&nbsp;sometimes takes the same value as $b1$ (eg. when $x = y$).&nbsp;</p><p>$b1 \\Leftrightarrow b3$ is a <em>Contingency</em>.</p><p>So, $b3$&nbsp;<strong>SOMETIMES</strong>&nbsp;takes the same value as $b1$.</p><hr><p>$b4&nbsp;\\equiv \\neg(\\neg Q(x,z) \\land&nbsp;Q(y,z)) \\equiv Q(x,z) \\lor \\neg Q(y,z)$</p><p>If $x &gt; z$ and $y &gt;= z$ then $b1$ and $b4$ both are true.<br>If $x=z$ then $b4$ is true but $b1$ is false.</p><p>$b1 \\Leftrightarrow b4$ is a <em>Contingency</em>.&nbsp;</p><p>So, $b4$ <strong>SOMETIMES</strong>&nbsp;takes the same value as $b1$.</p><hr><p>$<br>\\begin{align*}<br>b5 &amp;\\equiv [P(x,z) \\land P(y,z)] \\lor [P(x,z)&nbsp;\\land (\\ Q(y,z) \\land \\neg P(y,z)\\ )] \\\\<br>&amp;\\equiv P(x,z) \\land[P(y,z) \\lor (\\ Q(y,z) \\land \\neg P(y,z)\\ )]&nbsp; &amp;\\text{[Distributive property]} \\\\<br>&amp;\\equiv P(x,z) \\land [\\ \\underbrace{\\underbrace{P(y,z)}_{\\Large y\\ &gt;\\ z} \\lor \\underbrace{Q(y,z)}_{\\Large y\\ \\ge\\ z}}_{\\Large y\\ \\ge\\ z \\ =\\ Q(y,\\ z)}\\ ]&nbsp; &amp;[\\because A \\lor (\\neg A \\land B) \\equiv A \\lor &nbsp;B] \\\\<br>b5 &amp;\\equiv&nbsp;P(x,z) \\land Q(y,z) \\equiv b1&nbsp;\\\\<br>\\end{align*}<br>$</p><p>$b1 \\Leftrightarrow b5$ is&nbsp;<em>Valid</em>.</p><p>So, $b5$&nbsp;<strong>ALWAYS&nbsp;</strong>takes the same value as $b1$.</p><hr><p>$\\bf \\therefore \\text{Ans: B, C}$</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 27)</small></h4><a name=\"373150\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/16/162416fc8a5c235e78db.jpg\">Variables b2,b3,b4b2,b3,b4, and b5b5 ALWAYS, SOMETIMES, or NEVER takes the same value as b1b1.<br>\nWhich of the variable(s) SOMETIMES takes the same value as b1b1?</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 17)</small></h4><a name=\"373139\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/53/532e240d3da3466b17ad.jpg\"></p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:decimal\" type=\"1\">\n\t<li>from the above image we can say that, <strong>b1 and b2 will never be the same.</strong></li>\n\t<li>from the above image we can say that, when (x!=z); (y!=z) then value of b1 and b3 will be same ie <strong>sometimes b1 and b3 will&nbsp;be same.</strong></li>\n\t<li>from the above image we can say that, <strong>sometimes b1 and b4 will be same.</strong>&nbsp;One of such conditions is when (x&lt;z) and (y&gt;=z) both will be 0.</li>\n\t<li>from the above image we can say that,<strong> b1 and b5&nbsp;will always&nbsp;be the same.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>ANSWER : –&nbsp;B, C</strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 8)</small></h4><a name=\"428356\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">Same logical approach but in a visual way using truth table<br>\n<br>\nSince all of b1,b2,b3,b4,b5 only depends on x,y,z variable and only logical operations involved<br>\n<br>\nevery boolean expression = boolean function. Here b1,b2,b3,b4,b5 = f(x,y,z)<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\n<br>\nso we can approach this as<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\n<br>\nX &nbsp;Y &nbsp;Z &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;b1 &nbsp;b2 &nbsp;b3 &nbsp;b4 &nbsp;b5<br>\n<br>\n0 &nbsp;0 &nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0<br>\n<br>\n0 &nbsp;0 &nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0<br>\n<br>\n0 &nbsp;1 &nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0<br>\n<br>\n0 &nbsp;1 &nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0<br>\n<br>\n1 &nbsp;0 &nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1<br>\n<br>\n1 &nbsp;0 &nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0<br>\n<br>\n1 &nbsp;1 &nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1<br>\n<br>\n1 &nbsp;1 &nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\n<br>\nFrom the truth table we can clearly see that<br>\n<br>\nb1==b5 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=&gt; ALWAYS equals<br>\n<br>\nb1==compliment(b2) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=&gt; NEVER &nbsp;equals<br>\n<br>\nb1!=b3!=b4 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=&gt; &nbsp;but sometimes b1==b3(1 &nbsp;1 &nbsp;0) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;, b1==b4(1 0 0 &nbsp;or 1 &nbsp;1 &nbsp;0)<br>\n<br>\nSo the question asked SOMETIMES only =&gt; Answer: &nbsp;b3,b4</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"443600\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>$\\textbf{b1 = F} \\quad$ if $\\quad \\textbf{(x&gt;z) == F}$</p><p>$\\textbf{b1 = (y &gt;= z)} \\quad$ if $\\quad \\textbf{(x&gt;z) == T}$</p><hr><p>$\\textbf{b2 = T} \\quad$ if $\\quad (x&lt;=z) == T \\quad$ or $\\quad \\textbf{(x&gt;z) == F}$</p><p>$\\textbf{b2 = (y &lt; z)} \\quad$ if $\\quad (x&lt;=z) == F \\quad$ or $\\quad \\textbf{(x&gt;z) == T}$</p><hr><p>$\\textbf{b3 =} \\, \\, !(y &lt;= z) \\equiv \\textbf{(y&gt;z)} \\quad$ if $\\quad (x&lt;z) == T \\quad$ or $\\quad \\textbf{(x&gt;=z) == F}$</p><p>$\\textbf{b3 = F} \\quad$ if $\\quad (x&lt;z) == F \\quad$ or $\\quad \\textbf{(x&gt;=z) == T}$</p><hr><p>$\\textbf{b4 =} \\, \\, !(y &gt;= z) \\equiv \\textbf{(y &lt; z)} \\quad$ if $\\quad (x&lt;z) == T \\quad$ or $\\quad \\textbf{(x&gt;=z) == F}$</p><p>$\\textbf{b4 = T} \\quad$ if $\\quad (x&lt;z) == F \\quad$ or $\\quad \\textbf{(x&gt;=z) == T}$</p><hr><p>$\\textbf{b5 = F} \\quad$ if $\\quad \\textbf{(x&gt;z) == F}$</p><p>$\\textbf{b5 =} \\, ((y &gt; z) || (y==z)) \\equiv \\textbf{(y&gt;=z)} \\quad$ if $\\quad \\textbf{(x&gt;z) == T}$</p><hr><p>here,</p><ol><li>$b1$ and $b2$ are always opposite to each other for the same values of $x, y$ and $z$.</li><li>$b1$ and $b5$ are always same to each other for the same values of $x, y$ and $z$.</li><li>$b1$ and $b3$ are equal for values such that $y&lt;z$ and not equal for values such that $y=z$.</li><li>$b1$ and $b4$ are equal for values such that $y=z$ and not equal for values such that $y&lt;z$.</li><li>$b3$ and $b4$ are equal for values such that $x&gt;=z$ and not equal for values such that $x&lt;z$.</li></ol></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 6 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"534096\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><ol><li>METHOD-1<br><a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://beta.fileverse.io/files/97d4f21c-1638-476b-a4e6-9b771ed0b964\" target=\"_blank\">method-1 image</a></li><li><hr>METHOD-2<br><img alt=\"\" width=\"550\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\" src=\"denied:blob:https://gateoverflow.in/a7150906-c2d0-4b0b-82ac-66bf5910973b\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://beta.fileverse.io/files/795e0e76-626c-4acd-af8b-b749c85557c4\" target=\"_blank\">method-2 image</a></li></ol><p>Answer :$B,C$</p><p>Note: Pay attention on the word SOMETIMES.&nbsp;</p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/373092\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":14,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following two .$c$ files which both include the same $.h$ file:</p><p><img alt=\"\" height=\"410\" width=\"554\" src=\"media/47/47c7edfbd4138b6bbed3.png\"></p><p><br>When compiled, linked, and executed, the following output results:<br>$x = 1 \\; y = 2\\; z = 3\\; w = 4\\; v = 5$<br>Assuming that this program compiled and linked successfully, and based on the output shown above, add static and/or extern modifiers to the blank lines $(1)$ through $(10)$.<br><br>Leave them blank if neither modifier would be appropriate.<br><br>For example, if we say line $(2)$ is static then we consider line $(2)$ as $\\text{“static int x”}$. Or if we say line $(9)$ is empty then we treat line $9$ as $\\text{“int w = 4”}$.</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<br>Line$(1)$ – empty<br>Line$(2)$ - static<br>Line$(3)$ - empty<br>Line$(4)$ - extern<br>Line$(5)$ – empty<br>Line$(6)$ - empty<br>Line$(7)$ - empty<br>Line$(8)$ - static<br>Line$(9)$ - empty<br>Line$(10)$ - static"},{"key":"B","html":"<br>Line$(1)$ – static<br>Line$(2)$ - static<br>Line$(3)$ - extern<br>Line$(4)$ - empty<br>Line$(5)$ – extern<br>Line$(6)$ - empty<br>Line$(7)$ - extern<br>Line$(8)$ - static<br>Line$(9)$ - empty<br>Line$(10)$ - static"},{"key":"C","html":"<br>Line$(1)$ – static<br>Line$(2)$ - static<br>Line$(3)$ - empty<br>Line$(4)$ - empty<br>Line$(5)$ – empty<br>Line$(6)$ - empty<br>Line$(7)$ - empty<br>Line$(8)$ - static<br>Line$(9)$ - empty<br>Line$(10)$ - static"},{"key":"D","html":"<br>Line$(1)$ – empty<br>Line$(2)$ - static<br>Line$(3)$ - empty<br>Line$(4)$ - empty<br>Line$(5)$ – extern<br>Line$(6)$ - empty<br>Line$(7)$ - empty<br>Line$(8)$ - static<br>Line$(9)$ - empty<br>Line$(10)$ - static"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Selected solution <small>(score: 14)</small></h4><a name=\"375174\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>(1)</strong> Needs to be static, or else linking fails due to 2 conflicting strong symbols ‘inc’ with an “already defined” error</p>\n\n<p><strong>(2)</strong> x must be static. If it weren’t, and a.c and b.c shared the same x, its value would be 2, not 1.</p>\n\n<p><strong>(3)</strong> y should be extern (it must be shared between a.c and b.c, else its value wouldn’t be 2.) However, leaving it without modifier works also (For what its worth, int y; followed by int y = 1; compiles and the strong definition is used.) It cannot be static.</p>\n\n<p><strong>(4)</strong> z’s modifier must be blank so that ‘z’ becomes a common symbol (shared, but weak). If extern were used, z would be nowhere defined (undefined reference error). If static were used, it would have the value 4, not 3. Note that defining ‘z’ in a header file is generally considered bad practice.</p>\n\n<p><strong>(5)</strong> Should be extern (or left blank, which has identical semantics for functions.) If it were static, ‘b’ would be undefined in a.c</p>\n\n<p><strong>(6)</strong> Must be blank – static would conflict with the earlier declaration of y in the header file. Extern, which declares a variable, is incompatible with an initialization, which can only be used with a definition.</p>\n\n<p><strong>(7)</strong> Like (3), can be extern or blank. Note that neither choice is good design. If extern, the declaration should appear in a header. If blank, the definition is redundant (and will be dominated by b.c’s strong definition of w.</p>\n\n<p><strong>(8)</strong> Must be static since the value printed is 5 – a.c has its own copy of ‘v’</p>\n\n<p><strong>(9)</strong> Must be blank so that ‘w’ is defined globally. If static, a.o would have an resolved reference. Like (6), you cannot use extern with a definition.</p>\n\n<p><strong>(10)</strong> Could be blank or static. b.c could operate on a global symbol for ‘v’, or have a local copy – either way, it will not affect the output. Like (9), it cannot be extern.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/59/598e27535b12bf1255c5.png\"></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 12)</small></h4><a name=\"373159\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>The preprocessor generates a modified version of the source code, which includes the content of the header files and the values of the macros. This happens prior to compilation.&nbsp;<br>\n<br>\nIn the given source code, after the preprocessing stage, the contents of&nbsp;“a.h” will be available to both “a.c” and “b.c”.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Line (1): ___ int inc (int x) { .. };<br>\nThis has to&nbsp;be&nbsp;<strong>Static</strong>.&nbsp; Since this is inside header file, it will be copied into the modified source code, before compilation. So there will be two copies of the same function, one in each file.<br>\nIf we have it Empty or Extern, then during the linking process, Linker will not be able to distinguish the two functions and it will result in ambiguity / name conflict.&nbsp;<br>\nSo, if we have it as static, then we are making sure this function is not visible for linking.&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Line (2): ___ int x;<br>\nThis cannot be extern because there is no other definition anywhere.<br>\nThis may be <strong>Static or Global</strong>. During execution, the value of x remains unaffected, this is because both “a.c” and “b.c” will be working with its own version of “a.h”.</p>\n\n<p>Line (3): ___ int y;<br>\nIn “a.c”,&nbsp; y is declared globally in Line (6) and assigned a value 1.&nbsp; So, line (3) has to be <strong>extern</strong> to avoid conflicts.<br>\nDuring execution, the value of y changes from 1 to 2, this happens because call to b() increments y. From&nbsp;“b.c” point of view, y is declared as extern, which refers to the definition at Line(6) in “a.c”.&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Line (4): ___ int z;<br>\nThis cannot be extern because there is no other definition anywhere.<br>\nThis cannot be static, because we know static variables are not available for linking. But here, we see the value of z is made 4 by “a.c” and next it is decremented by “b.c”, and final result is 3. For this to happen, z must be <strong>global (empty).</strong></p>\n\n<p>Line (5): ___ void b(void);<br>\nFunction void b(void) is being used externally inside of “a.c”&nbsp;&nbsp;while it’s definition is in “b.c”. For the declaration, we may have it as <strong>Extern or Empty.</strong>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Line (6): ___ int y = 1;<br>\nThis has to be <strong>Global (empty)</strong>, since Line (3) is extern.</p>\n\n<p>Line (7): ___ int w;<br>\nThe value of w is defined in “b.c” even though it is being used in “a.c”. So, it has to be declared as <strong>extern.</strong></p>\n\n<p>Line (8): ___ int v=5;<br>\nThe variable v in “a.c” is initialized to 5, and is not affected by b(). So it must be declared as&nbsp;<strong>Static.</strong></p>\n\n<p>Line (9): ___ int w=4;<br>\nThe variable w is being initialized&nbsp;here, which is being referred externally by “a.c”.&nbsp;So it must be <strong>global (empty).</strong>&nbsp; &nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Line (10): ___ int v=5;<br>\nThe variable&nbsp;v in “b.c” is independent of v in “a.c”. So it may be declared as&nbsp;<strong>Static or Global.<br>\n<br>\nAnswer: B</strong><br>\n&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 7)</small></h4><a name=\"373160\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Answer : B</strong><br>\n1. <strong>static</strong> - function definition, cannot be left empty or made extern, so as to avoid name conflict.<br>\n2. <strong>static</strong> - from the result we can conclude both x's are different in file a.c and b.c<br>\n3. <strong>extern</strong> - since y is defined in file a.c and is being used by function in file b.c (from output we can conclude it)<br>\n4.&nbsp;<strong>empty&nbsp;</strong>- it can’t be made extern since there’s no other definition of z and will cause undefined reference error. it can’t be made static also since it is being used in file b.c&nbsp;(from output we can conclude it)<br>\n5. <strong>extern/empty</strong> - function declaration<br>\n6. <strong>extern/empty</strong> - must not be made static since y is being used by another file b.c<br>\n7. <strong>extern</strong> - since w is defined in file b.c<br>\n8. <strong>static</strong> - since this v is not same as that of file b.c<br>\n9. <strong>extern/empty</strong> - must not be made static since w is being used by another file a.c<br>\n10. <strong>static</strong> - since this v is not same as that of file a.c</p>\n\n<p>In 6 and 9, we can also use extern because when used with definitions extern also creates memory for that variable, but compiler will give warning.</p>\n\n<p>Answer :- <strong>B</strong></p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 4 <small>(score: 2)</small></h4><a name=\"428354\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">option elimination at time of exam is best i think.<br>\n<br>\nsince 'w' need to be &nbsp;4<br>\n<br>\nThere is only one way to fullfill this <br>\n<br>\nLine(7) should be extern and only option B fullfill this &nbsp;so all others can be eliminated</div></section><section><h4>Source solution 5 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"477974\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">A quick lookover <br>\nwhen you see b.c line 7.<br>\nwe want w to be 4 and this line is fullfilling our case.<br>\nso just , extern line 7.<br>\nno other option matches .<br>\nso B .<br>\n<br>\nThis was luck, if not , go systematic.</div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/373090\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":15,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following variable and function definitions:</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">int g=10;\nint q3() {\n    static int g=5;\n    return ++g;\n}\nint q4() {\n    extern int g;\n    return ++g;\n}\nint q5() {\n    int g=1;\n    return ++g;\n}</pre><p>What is the value of the expression $q3() + q3() + q4() + q4() + q5() + q5()?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$32$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$34$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$38$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$40$</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><div class=\"source-solutions\" data-answer-source=\"inferred\"><section><h4>Source solution 1 <small>(score: 16)</small></h4><a name=\"373143\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>Answer: 40</strong></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Points to note/remember:</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:decimal\" type=\"1\"><li>Static variables are stored in static memory and hence even if activation record of a function is popped the value of static variable persists Global variables are also stored in static memory.</li><li>Although associativity of addition operator is defined (L&gt;R) and</li></ol><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">q3()+q3()+q4()+q4()+q5()+q5()</pre><p>will be interpreted as</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">((((q3() + q3()) + q4()) + q4()) + q5()) + q5()</pre><p>the final addition can only be done after the values are returned, but the order in which the following functions are evaluated is not defined. So in (q3() + q4()) for example we can say for sure which among q3 and q4 which one is evaluated first. This could be concerning because of static variable since value of variable persists between function calls and it may lead to race condition (final result depends on order of evaluation). This however is not concerning in this question as we’re just adding the result and addition is commutative but if this were subtraction then the result may not be consistent among different compilers.</p><ol start=\"3\" style=\"list-style-type:decimal\" type=\"1\"><li>Extern int g in function q4 refers to global int g.</li></ol><p>So,</p><p>q3 runs and initialize it’s local static int g to 5, make it 6 and returns (pre increment), now again when q3 runs, read the static value (static variables are initialized only once) as 6, make it 7 and returns.</p><p>q4 runs and does basically same but with global variable and 11 and 12.</p><p>q5 runs and initialize local int var g to 1 and make it 2 and returns. But since the variable was not static it was stored in the activation record of q5, which (AR) was destroyed/popped right when q5 returned. Now when q5 runs again repeats itself, initialize, add, returns 2.</p><p>Hence the expression</p><pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">q3()+q3()+q4()+q4()+q5()+q5()\n</pre><p>becomes 6 + 7 + 11 + 12 + 2 + 2 = 40</p><p>&nbsp;</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 2 <small>(score: 10)</small></h4><a name=\"373115\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>ANSWER : 40</strong></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Expression to evaluate : q3() + q3() + q4() + q4() + q5() + q5()<br>\n<br>\ngiven, global int g = 10<br>\n<br>\nnow when q3() is called,<br>\n<br>\nstatic int g = 5 is defined, and return ++g will return 6 and increment static variable g of q3 to 6.<br>\n<br>\nso now our expression is : 6 + q3() + q4() + q4() + q5() + q5()<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\n<br>\nagain, q3() is called,<br>\n<br>\nnow since g inside of q3 is static it won’t be initialized again, therefore return ++g will return 7 and increment g of q3 to 7<br>\n<br>\nso now our expression is : 6 + 7 + q4() + q4() + q5() + q5()<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\nnow q4 is called,<br>\n<br>\nit has extern int g which will refer to global g which has value 10, return ++g will return 11 and increment global g to 11.<br>\n<br>\nso now our expression is : 6 + 7 + 11 + q4() + q5() + q5()<br>\n<br>\nand global g = 11<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<br>\n<br>\nagain q4 is called, and return ++g will return 12 and increment global g to 12.<br>\n<br>\nso now our expression is : 6 + 7 + 11 + 12 + q5() + q5()<br>\n<br>\nand global g = 12<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\n<br>\nnow q5 is called,<br>\n<br>\nit has local g = 1, return ++g will return 2 and increment g to 2 but it will be destroyed as end of function is reached.<br>\n<br>\nso now our expression is : 6 + 7 + 11 + 12 + 2 + q5()<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\n<br>\nagain q5 is called,<br>\n<br>\nagain g = 1 will be initialized as it is local variable and ++g will return 2 and g becomes 2 and get destroyed.<br>\n<br>\nso now our expression is : 6 + 7 + 11 + 12 + 2 + 2<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\ntherefore, our expression becomes : 6 + 7 + 11 + 12 + 2 + 2 = 40 ( ANSWER)<br>\n<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\nNOTE : the order in which functions are called here does not matter, the answer remains same.</p></div></section><section><h4>Source solution 3 <small>(score: 1)</small></h4><a name=\"377321\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><em>q</em>3( ) :</p>\n\n<p>static int g = 5 // This will create it’s own local static copy of g</p>\n\n<p>return ++g // Uses it’s own static local copy. So, first the value will be incremented and then returned i.e = 6</p>\n\n<p><em>q</em>3( ) :</p>\n\n<p>static int g = 5 // This doesn’t gets executed as static variables are executed only once.</p>\n\n<p>return ++g // It uses the local copy of g which was executed in the first call i.e = 7</p>\n\n<p><em>q</em>4( ) :</p>\n\n<p>extern int g; // It simply references to a global variable created at the beginning of program.</p>\n\n<p>return ++g; // The global g is accessed and pre incremented i.e = 11</p>\n\n<p><em>q</em>4( ) :</p>\n\n<p>extern int g; // Again it simply references to a global variable created at the beginning of program.</p>\n\n<p>return ++g; // The global g is accessed and pre incremented i.e = 12</p>\n\n<p><em>q</em>5( ) :</p>\n\n<p>int g = 1; // It creates its own local copy the value of g is initialized to 1. So, here g = 1</p>\n\n<p>return ++g; // It uses the local copy of g and returns pre incremented value i.e = 2</p>\n\n<p><em>q</em>5( ) :</p>\n\n<p>int g = 1; // Again it creates its own local copy the value of g is again initialized to 1. So, here again g = 1</p>\n\n<p>return ++g; // It uses the local copy of g and returns pre incremented value i.e = 2</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong><em>q</em>3()+<em>q</em>3()+<em>q</em>4()+<em>q</em>4()+<em>q</em>5()+<em>q</em>5()&nbsp; = 6 + 7 + 11 + 12 + 2 + 2 = 40 (Ans)</strong></p></div></section></div><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/373089\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"}],"sectionsMeta":[{"name":"Technical","marks":20.0,"instructions":null}],"math":true,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"Technical","start":0,"count":15}],"stats":{"questions":15,"maxMarks":19,"types":{"MCQ":8,"MSQ":7},"withoutKey":1}};
